    {
      "id": "kf9Wti-chDWbVRVPML-c0",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 8692.510954361356,
      "y": -4365.2456629295975,
      "width": 8137.79052734375,
      "height": 675,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bCI",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 756672365,
      "version": 31,
      "versionNonce": 38862285,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786443976072,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "POST /tools/find-next-opening — given a date range (and optionally a specific doctor), it returns the earliest bookable appointment slot. It's the \"when can I get in?\" query that sits between \"who are our doctors\" (get-doctor) and \"book me an appointment\" (not yet built).\n\nWhy it comes after the doctor route\nThe doctor endpoints (get-doctor, and the doctors table) answer \"does this doctor exist / who is it.\" Availability search builds directly on top of that identity layer — it needs a doctor's working hours, time off, and existing bookings before it can answer \"is there a free slot.\" So logically: doctors → their schedules → availability search → booking is the build order, and this route is the schedule layer.\n\nHow to explain the behavior (3 bullets)\nSearch window, not a single day — caller gives dateFrom/dateTo (and duration), and it walks day by day until it finds the first open slot, rather than requiring the caller to guess a date and retry.\nOne doctor or \"any doctor\" — if doctorId is omitted, it searches across every doctor and returns whichever has the earliest opening. Good for \"I don't care who, just get me in soonest\" callers (e.g. a voice agent).\n\"Available\" = works that day AND has no time off AND no conflicting appointment — this is computed from three data sources merged together (doctor_working_hours, doctor_time_off, appointments), not stored anywhere directly.\nDesign points worth calling out\nSingle DB round trip per day, not per-slot — it fetches working hours + time off + bookings once per candidate doctor per day, then does the slot math (_compute_slots) in Python. Keeps DB load predictable regardless of appointment duration.\nShared logic with future booking — _get_working_window (works-this-day + no-time-off check) is written so book-appointment can reuse it later, so \"is this slot valid\" is defined in exactly one place instead of being re-derived and potentially drifting.\nDefensive on bad input — malformed dates return a clean {\"found\": false, \"reason\": ...} instead of a 500, since dates here ultimately come from an LLM parsing speech, not a strict form.\nLogged like the other routes — entry + every outcome (invalid date, no doctor, no doctors on file, found, exhausted window) so failures are traceable in production the same way find-patient/register-patient already are.\nIf you want, I can turn this into a short README/API-docs section for the service, or a one-slide diagram of the data flow (doctor_working_hours + doctor_time_off + appointments → _compute_slots → response).",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "POST /tools/find-next-opening — given a date range (and optionally a specific doctor), it returns the earliest bookable appointment slot. It's the \"when can I get in?\" query that sits between \"who are our doctors\" (get-doctor) and \"book me an appointment\" (not yet built).\n\nWhy it comes after the doctor route\nThe doctor endpoints (get-doctor, and the doctors table) answer \"does this doctor exist / who is it.\" Availability search builds directly on top of that identity layer — it needs a doctor's working hours, time off, and existing bookings before it can answer \"is there a free slot.\" So logically: doctors → their schedules → availability search → booking is the build order, and this route is the schedule layer.\n\nHow to explain the behavior (3 bullets)\nSearch window, not a single day — caller gives dateFrom/dateTo (and duration), and it walks day by day until it finds the first open slot, rather than requiring the caller to guess a date and retry.\nOne doctor or \"any doctor\" — if doctorId is omitted, it searches across every doctor and returns whichever has the earliest opening. Good for \"I don't care who, just get me in soonest\" callers (e.g. a voice agent).\n\"Available\" = works that day AND has no time off AND no conflicting appointment — this is computed from three data sources merged together (doctor_working_hours, doctor_time_off, appointments), not stored anywhere directly.\nDesign points worth calling out\nSingle DB round trip per day, not per-slot — it fetches working hours + time off + bookings once per candidate doctor per day, then does the slot math (_compute_slots) in Python. Keeps DB load predictable regardless of appointment duration.\nShared logic with future booking — _get_working_window (works-this-day + no-time-off check) is written so book-appointment can reuse it later, so \"is this slot valid\" is defined in exactly one place instead of being re-derived and potentially drifting.\nDefensive on bad input — malformed dates return a clean {\"found\": false, \"reason\": ...} instead of a 500, since dates here ultimately come from an LLM parsing speech, not a strict form.\nLogged like the other routes — entry + every outcome (invalid date, no doctor, no doctors on file, found, exhausted window) so failures are traceable in production the same way find-patient/register-patient already are.\nIf you want, I can turn this into a short README/API-docs section for the service, or a one-slide diagram of the data flow (doctor_working_hours + doctor_time_off + appointments → _compute_slots → response).",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "veGvNLAN3o9j3hWOyjQzA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 12469.993925715255,
      "y": -5114.735958615601,
      "width": 2347.453857421875,
      "height": 130.13303570143566,
