      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "app/bedrock_stream.py\nThis is the core module that manages a bidirectional streaming session with Amazon Bedrock's Nova Sonic speech-to-speech model, \nacting as the \"brain\" of a voice AI receptionist for a dental clinic. \nKey pieces:\n\n1. System prompt — DEFAULT_SYSTEM_PROMPT_TEMPLATE is a detailed receptionist script \nfor \"Greenfield Dental Clinic\": identify returning vs. new patients, capture chief complaint, \nfind an appointment slot, collect patient details, book, and \nend the call — with explicit tool-calling instructions at each step.\n\n2. Event templates — Nova Sonic's bidirectional protocol is JSON-event-based \n(session start, prompt start, content start/end, audio input, text input, tool result, session end). \nThe module builds these as string templates and \na _start_prompt_event() function that also registers the tool schemas \n(findPatientTool, registerPatientTool, updatePatientPhoneTool, getDoctorTool, findNextOpeningTool, bookAppointmentTool, endCallTool) that the model can call.\n\n3. BedrockStreamManager class — the main orchestrator:\n\nOpens a bidirectional stream to Bedrock (invoke_model_with_bidirectional_stream), \nsends the session/prompt/system-prompt setup events.\n\nRuns two background tasks: \n_process_audio_input() (drains an input queue, base64-encodes caller audio, sends it upstream) and \n_process_responses() (reads Bedrock's streamed events — transcripts, audio output, tool-use requests — and routes them).\n\nOn a toolUse/contentEnd event, it dispatches to handle_tool_request(), \nwhich runs the tool asynchronously via an injected tool_executor callable (kept decoupled from main.py/the booking service — this module has no direct HTTP dependency) and sends the result back into the stream.\nendCallTool is handled specially inline — it just tells the model to say goodbye and sets call_should_end.\nclose() tears everything down in the correct order (cancel pending tool tasks, end audio content, end prompt, end session, close the stream, await the response task) to avoid Bedrock validation errors from double-closing content blocks.\ntest_bedrock_stream.py\nA manual/smoke-test script (not a pytest suite) that exercises BedrockStreamManager end-to-end against the real Bedrock API:\n\nGenerates 2 seconds of a synthetic 440Hz sine tone as 16kHz PCM16 audio.\nCreates a manager with a stub_tool_executor (any tool call just returns an error — tool dispatch isn't wired up yet, per a \"Stage C\" comment).\nConnects, starts an audio content block, feeds in the tone.\nReads events off output_queue for up to 8s per event, printing event types until completionEnd or timeout.\nDrains any returned audio into a .wav file (bedrock_test_output.wav) so you can listen to what Nova Sonic said back.\nIt's essentially a hand-run integration check: \"does the stream connect, accept audio, and produce a spoken response?\" — useful for verifying the low-level plumbing in bedrock_stream.py works before wiring in the real tool executor and WebSocket/telephony layer (per TESTING.md alongside it).",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "app/bedrock_stream.py\nThis is the core module that manages a bidirectional streaming session with Amazon Bedrock's Nova Sonic speech-to-speech model, \nacting as the \"brain\" of a voice AI receptionist for a dental clinic. \nKey pieces:\n\n1. System prompt — DEFAULT_SYSTEM_PROMPT_TEMPLATE is a detailed receptionist script \nfor \"Greenfield Dental Clinic\": identify returning vs. new patients, capture chief complaint, \nfind an appointment slot, collect patient details, book, and \nend the call — with explicit tool-calling instructions at each step.\n\n2. Event templates — Nova Sonic's bidirectional protocol is JSON-event-based \n(session start, prompt start, content start/end, audio input, text input, tool result, session end). \nThe module builds these as string templates and \na _start_prompt_event() function that also registers the tool schemas \n(findPatientTool, registerPatientTool, updatePatientPhoneTool, getDoctorTool, findNextOpeningTool, bookAppointmentTool, endCallTool) that the model can call.\n\n3. BedrockStreamManager class — the main orchestrator:\n\nOpens a bidirectional stream to Bedrock (invoke_model_with_bidirectional_stream), \nsends the session/prompt/system-prompt setup events.\n\nRuns two background tasks: \n_process_audio_input() (drains an input queue, base64-encodes caller audio, sends it upstream) and \n_process_responses() (reads Bedrock's streamed events — transcripts, audio output, tool-use requests — and routes them).\n\nOn a toolUse/contentEnd event, it dispatches to handle_tool_request(), \nwhich runs the tool asynchronously via an injected tool_executor callable (kept decoupled from main.py/the booking service — this module has no direct HTTP dependency) and sends the result back into the stream.\nendCallTool is handled specially inline — it just tells the model to say goodbye and sets call_should_end.\nclose() tears everything down in the correct order (cancel pending tool tasks, end audio content, end prompt, end session, close the stream, await the response task) to avoid Bedrock validation errors from double-closing content blocks.\ntest_bedrock_stream.py\nA manual/smoke-test script (not a pytest suite) that exercises BedrockStreamManager end-to-end against the real Bedrock API:\n\nGenerates 2 seconds of a synthetic 440Hz sine tone as 16kHz PCM16 audio.\nCreates a manager with a stub_tool_executor (any tool call just returns an error — tool dispatch isn't wired up yet, per a \"Stage C\" comment).\nConnects, starts an audio content block, feeds in the tone.\nReads events off output_queue for up to 8s per event, printing event types until completionEnd or timeout.\nDrains any returned audio into a .wav file (bedrock_test_output.wav) so you can listen to what Nova Sonic said back.\nIt's essentially a hand-run integration check: \"does the stream connect, accept audio, and produce a spoken response?\" — useful for verifying the low-level plumbing in bedrock_stream.py works before wiring in the real tool executor and WebSocket/telephony layer (per TESTING.md alongside it).",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
